00:20:40.138 --> 00:20:42.541
and they weren't liquid
enough to meet the demand

00:20:42.541 --> 00:20:43.742
from certain depositors.

00:20:44.943 --> 00:20:46.511
I think that's obvious.

00:20:46.511 --> 00:20:49.248
But it's all the spending
that drove the inflation.

00:20:49.248 --> 00:20:50.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, it's like
literally record spending,

00:20:50.816 --> 00:20:53.051
and of course his budget,
the President's budget,

00:20:53.051 --> 00:20:54.820
we were at record
levels of spending,

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.089 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he increased it even more
with his proposed budget.

00:20:57.089 --> 00:20:59.324
So we've got to get
a handle on that.

00:20:59.324 --> 00:21:00.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
And frankly, I think
we've got to get in place,

00:21:00.859 --> 00:21:03.729
and I've pushed for this,
the idea that when we get

00:21:03.729 --> 00:21:05.497
to the end of the fiscal year,

00:21:05.497 --> 00:21:07.799
we can't have this
shutdown scenario.

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:10.769 align:start size:97% position:3%
So what I want is a no
shutdown act so that, okay,

00:21:10.769 --> 00:21:13.472
